1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
*music*

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:59,980
From preaching so we might be here a while, just kidding, I'm just kidding mostly, we got to beat the Methodists to Mother's Day restaurants, alright, I don't know why the Methodists got astray there, that's not fair, we are in week two of a series on the book of Jonah.

3
00:01:00,620 --> 00:01:20,360
And I think Jonah for many of us is a familiar story, but as somebody who grew up in the church and have heard the story of Jonah over and over again as I've studied the text, I want to make the argument that I think the story of Jonah is familiar, but the text of Scripture called the book of Jonah is far less familiar.

4
00:01:21,020 --> 00:01:37,640
It is a complex and sophisticated text filled with irony, confusing words and phrases, there's so much to be mined in the book of Jonah that we often miss because we've heard the story so many times that we think we're familiar with it, that we just breeze past it rather than study it.

5
00:01:37,640 --> 00:01:48,260
And Kent did a really good job last week of talking about all of the different challenging parts of the text and ways in which the text really is communicating so much and just the way that it's structured.

6
00:01:48,260 --> 00:02:00,260
And I think as Western intellectuals, we have a way of looking at some of these ancient texts with kind of our nose up at them and we try and explain away some things that seem confusing to us.

7
00:02:00,880 --> 00:02:06,140
Anything where it's like out of place, it's like, oh, well, they just didn't know how the world worked.

8
00:02:06,140 --> 00:02:08,920
And it's like, no, this is the Scriptures.

9
00:02:08,920 --> 00:02:10,800
It is from God through humans.

10
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:16,120
It is an extremely clear and sophisticated text that is even communicating to us.

11
00:02:16,120 --> 00:02:23,140
When things look out of place, we as Westerners, we try and explain it away and come up with a really clean answer for why it's written the way that it is.

12
00:02:23,140 --> 00:02:27,360
And we have to realize that we're actually interacting with an Eastern text.

13
00:02:27,360 --> 00:02:36,820
And when Easterners come across problems in a text like the Scripture, their solution is not to just explain it away or come up with a clear and clean answer.

14
00:02:36,820 --> 00:02:47,640
Their solution is actually to search out the inconsistencies, search out the confusing parts, because there's something in the confusion that the author is inviting us deeper into.

15
00:02:47,640 --> 00:02:49,400
The rabbis actually have a phrase for it.

16
00:02:49,400 --> 00:02:50,360
It's called darshene.

17
00:02:50,360 --> 00:02:51,160
Can you say that?

18
00:02:51,160 --> 00:02:53,100
I might be pronouncing it wrong.

19
00:02:53,100 --> 00:02:53,760
That's okay.

20
00:02:53,760 --> 00:02:55,980
It literally means search me out.

21
00:02:55,980 --> 00:03:00,480
It's like when we're in the Hebrew text and we come across a problem in the text.

22
00:03:00,480 --> 00:03:06,000
And I know as good American Christians who believe in the sufficiency of Scripture, we would say there's no problems in the text.

23
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:08,800
There's lots of problems in the text.

24
00:03:08,800 --> 00:03:14,520
And the problems in the text are an invitation to a lifetime of meditation on the character of God.

25
00:03:14,520 --> 00:03:23,280
I don't know how long it took me to realize that the people of God, the Israelites, were literally named after this pursuit.

26
00:03:23,280 --> 00:03:26,400
Israel literally means he who wrestles with God.

27
00:03:26,400 --> 00:03:34,860
And so I want to make the argument over the next couple of weeks, and I think Kent started us off really well, that the book of Jonah is a book that we're to be wrestling with.

28
00:03:34,860 --> 00:03:42,100
It's an invitation for us to really think about what God might want to teach us in his word.

29
00:03:42,880 --> 00:03:45,320
And I just, I want to say this up front.

30
00:03:45,320 --> 00:03:55,120
For those of you that might be getting stuck on the historicity of whether or not a real human got swallowed by a real whale, like, I might get fired for this.

31
00:03:57,520 --> 00:04:03,740
You can believe that it's a literary story meant to teach us things and be a good Christian.

32
00:04:03,740 --> 00:04:12,400
So if, like, you're, we believe in a resurrected Savior, so I think the thought of somebody living in a whale for a couple days is not that hard to, like, wrap our heads around.

33
00:04:12,900 --> 00:04:15,600
So both options are legitimate and valid.

34
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:19,900
But if you're so stuck on that that you can't hear the rest of the story, I just want to give you permission right now.

35
00:04:19,900 --> 00:04:21,080
Take a deep breath.

36
00:04:21,080 --> 00:04:28,480
You can still be a good Christian and believe that this book was a story written to God's people to teach them spiritual truth.

37
00:04:28,480 --> 00:04:30,320
Cool.

38
00:04:30,320 --> 00:04:30,800
You good?

39
00:04:31,400 --> 00:04:32,380
Am I fired yet?

40
00:04:32,380 --> 00:04:34,560
All right.

41
00:04:34,560 --> 00:04:35,060
We'll see.

42
00:04:35,060 --> 00:04:36,360
We'll see.

43
00:04:36,360 --> 00:04:36,640
All right.

44
00:04:36,640 --> 00:04:40,900
Jonah is a small book filled with a lifetime's worth of reflection and meditation.

45
00:04:40,900 --> 00:04:45,700
And the reason is because the story, the book of Jonah, isn't actually about a big fish.

46
00:04:45,700 --> 00:04:47,720
Kent said that last week.

47
00:04:47,720 --> 00:04:49,020
It's not about the city of Nineveh.

48
00:04:49,020 --> 00:04:50,600
It's not even about the prophet Jonah.

49
00:04:50,600 --> 00:04:52,660
It's about you and me.

50
00:04:52,660 --> 00:04:56,880
It's about our perspective on God's world and God's character.

51
00:04:56,880 --> 00:05:11,980
It forces us to wrestle with whether or not we're okay with a God who is compassionate and gracious, slow to anger and abounding in love and faithfulness both to us and to the worst people on the planet.

52
00:05:11,980 --> 00:05:22,740
To those that we would look upon with disdain and say they are not worthy of God's grace and compassion and forgiveness, but I am.

53
00:05:22,740 --> 00:05:30,980
The book of Jonah is about wrestling with that reality and who are the people that we have deemed unworthy of God's forgiveness and his grace.

54
00:05:30,980 --> 00:05:41,420
And where do we act like Jonah towards a group of people that we have said they are unworthy of the God who is compassionate and gracious, slow to anger and abounding in steadfast love.

55
00:05:42,080 --> 00:05:56,220
And I don't think there's a better text than all of the Scriptures to confront our current cultural moment, which is highly individualistic and charged with prideful, ideological, and nationalistic groups that are at odds with one another.

56
00:05:57,180 --> 00:06:08,700
And force us to view one another as enemies unworthy of God's grace and force ourselves to put ourselves on soapboxes where we are more worthy than others of the grace and forgiveness of God.

57
00:06:08,700 --> 00:06:14,740
So we might just preach the book of Jonah every year from now until whenever you guys do fire me.

58
00:06:16,620 --> 00:06:21,680
But for now, let's just go ahead and pray and dive into Jonah chapter 2.

59
00:06:21,680 --> 00:06:25,340
Thank you.

60
00:06:25,340 --> 00:06:25,980
Pun intended.

61
00:06:25,980 --> 00:06:31,340
We're going to dive into Jonah chapter 2.

62
00:06:31,340 --> 00:06:33,420
Thank you.

63
00:06:33,420 --> 00:06:33,900
Thank you.

64
00:06:33,900 --> 00:06:34,320
Thank you.

65
00:06:34,320 --> 00:06:37,580
I'll be here all week.

66
00:06:37,580 --> 00:06:38,240
All right, let's pray.

67
00:06:38,240 --> 00:06:40,000
Lord, help us.

68
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:40,600
We need to pray.

69
00:06:40,600 --> 00:06:55,120
Oh, Father, Jesus, Holy Spirit, God, we believe the Scriptures are your word given to us through your people, and they are, there is a lifetime worth of meditation in these texts.

70
00:06:55,120 --> 00:07:01,980
Book of Psalms says that blessed is the one who meditates on the law of the Lord day and night.

71
00:07:01,980 --> 00:07:09,380
For that produces deep roots and fruitfulness and faithfulness in our lives.

72
00:07:11,380 --> 00:07:28,440
God, Holy Spirit, I pray against any spirit of self-righteousness in the room that is already translating the meaning of the text in a way in which allows us to keep believing the things that we believe and living the way that we live without you confronting the hypocrisy in our hearts.

73
00:07:28,440 --> 00:07:33,840
So, God, I pray against the spirit of self-righteousness.

74
00:07:34,880 --> 00:07:36,680
I pray against the spirit of shame.

75
00:07:37,860 --> 00:07:50,120
Those who are in the room who feel like this is all well and good for some of us, but they've gone too far, they've strayed too far, that they're too far gone from God.

76
00:07:50,120 --> 00:07:53,660
I pray for, I pray against the spirit of shame, against the spirit of self-righteousness.

77
00:07:53,700 --> 00:07:56,900
And I pray that your Scriptures will confront us where we are at.

78
00:07:56,900 --> 00:08:01,840
And Holy Spirit, you would give each one of us individual invitations into deeper communion with you.

79
00:08:01,840 --> 00:08:05,260
Because you are a good and loving father.

80
00:08:05,260 --> 00:08:12,200
And your desire is that all people who were created in your image might know you more and more.

81
00:08:13,480 --> 00:08:17,280
So that your kingdom might become more clear on earth as it is in heaven.

82
00:08:17,280 --> 00:08:18,480
It's in Jesus' name I pray.

83
00:08:18,480 --> 00:08:19,480
Amen.

84
00:08:19,480 --> 00:08:24,600
If you want to follow along, we are going to be reading from Jonah chapter 2 when we get there.

85
00:08:24,600 --> 00:08:29,500
In 2020, does anybody want to revisit what happened in the years 2020?

86
00:08:29,500 --> 00:08:31,420
Me either.

87
00:08:31,420 --> 00:08:36,220
But in the year 2020, Netflix released a documentary called The Social Dilemma.

88
00:08:36,220 --> 00:08:38,480
Anybody watch this, The Social Dilemma?

89
00:08:39,440 --> 00:08:44,880
The Social Dilemma captured the cultural conversation of our time for a few months at least.

90
00:08:44,880 --> 00:08:46,400
Many of you have probably seen it.

91
00:08:46,400 --> 00:08:52,340
And if you haven't seen it, you've likely been exposed to the primary thesis of the documentary, whether you know it or not.

92
00:08:52,340 --> 00:08:57,360
This documentary was made by a lot of people who were involved in high-level tech company jobs.

93
00:08:57,360 --> 00:09:04,080
And the thesis of the documentary was that tech companies have employed the smartest people in the world.

94
00:09:04,080 --> 00:09:11,920
And they have spent billions and billions and billions of dollars.

95
00:09:11,920 --> 00:09:13,880
You guys are not laughing at my jokes today.

96
00:09:13,880 --> 00:09:14,260
It's okay.

97
00:09:14,260 --> 00:09:23,060
To create algorithms aimed at curating our feeds and our ads to keep us clicking, to keep us interested.

98
00:09:23,060 --> 00:09:31,740
And the things that they have realized that keep us the most interested is the things that make us angry and that reinforce our previously held beliefs.

99
00:09:31,740 --> 00:09:38,840
These algorithms, these algorithms, over time, they shrink our world down and cause us to stop seeing things with clarity.

100
00:09:38,840 --> 00:09:45,860
Because the majority of the content that you and I consume, and even if you're like, I'm not on social media, it's like, no, your phone is listening to you.

101
00:09:46,380 --> 00:09:51,300
And you're probably on the internet at some point in your life or YouTube.

102
00:09:51,300 --> 00:09:54,160
Those things are shrinking down your world.

103
00:09:54,160 --> 00:10:01,780
Because they are filtering all of the content that you see through your own self-interest.

104
00:10:01,780 --> 00:10:07,080
And they slowly, over time, have trained us to reinterpret the world through our own lenses.

105
00:10:09,020 --> 00:10:15,760
They force us into smaller and smaller ideological tribes filled with people who see the world as we see it.

106
00:10:15,760 --> 00:10:22,820
And they cause us to look at disdain with anyone who is outside of the tribe that we have identified with.

107
00:10:22,820 --> 00:10:30,720
These algorithms, they cause us to stop seeing God's world God's way and start seeing God's world our way.

108
00:10:31,620 --> 00:10:37,940
Our reality is distorted, and it leads us to become more and more self-righteous and judgmental.

109
00:10:37,940 --> 00:10:42,160
Am I the only person in the room that feels the weight of what I'm talking about this morning?

110
00:10:42,160 --> 00:10:52,720
Many of us have, we've got broken relationships with family members because of algorithms created by billionaire tech companies

111
00:10:52,720 --> 00:11:00,000
that have forced us to see the world through our own self-interest and our own self-focused lenses.

112
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:04,080
And the reason I share this story is because I think by the time we get to Jonah chapter 2,

113
00:11:04,080 --> 00:11:07,980
this is the very perspective that the prophet Jonah has.

114
00:11:07,980 --> 00:11:11,760
This is where I believe we find Jonah at the beginning of our story.

115
00:11:11,760 --> 00:11:17,180
Many of the pop culture depictions of the book of Jonah would like you to think that Jonah was afraid

116
00:11:17,180 --> 00:11:21,640
of what the Ninevites would do to him if he went and preached the word of God to them.

117
00:11:21,640 --> 00:11:23,300
But I don't see that anywhere in the text.

118
00:11:23,300 --> 00:11:27,200
What I actually see in the text is that Jonah actually hated the people of Nineveh.

119
00:11:27,200 --> 00:11:28,920
He wasn't afraid of them.

120
00:11:28,920 --> 00:11:33,960
He was more afraid of what God would do for them if he did the thing God asked him to do.

121
00:11:33,960 --> 00:11:40,760
And so what I think I see in the book of Jonah is not an afraid prophet,

122
00:11:40,760 --> 00:11:47,340
but a self-righteous and judgmental prophet who is good with God extending grace to him and his people,

123
00:11:47,340 --> 00:11:50,400
but not good with God extending grace to other people.

124
00:11:51,640 --> 00:12:00,500
And I do want to have a little bit of empathy for Jonah when we talk about this,

125
00:12:00,500 --> 00:12:05,700
because the people of Nineveh were horrible people, right?

126
00:12:05,700 --> 00:12:07,800
Like, let's just get this out in the open.

127
00:12:07,800 --> 00:12:16,520
Like, God is asking a prophet of the people of God to go to the worst people on the planet at this time,

128
00:12:16,520 --> 00:12:19,900
whose warfare was known throughout the world for being,

129
00:12:19,900 --> 00:12:26,120
like, I heard one scholar say that the Assyrians made the Romans look like child's play, right?

130
00:12:26,120 --> 00:12:28,920
Like, these are horrible, evil, wicked people.

131
00:12:29,020 --> 00:12:32,220
And so you can have a little bit of empathy for Jonah looking at God and saying,

132
00:12:32,220 --> 00:12:34,360
seriously, these people?

133
00:12:34,360 --> 00:12:37,520
So we've got a little bit of empathy here,

134
00:12:37,520 --> 00:12:41,500
but at the same time, God is confronting Jonah in this posture.

135
00:12:41,500 --> 00:12:45,160
And I think he wants to confront each and every one of us who have a similar posture

136
00:12:45,160 --> 00:12:49,800
to a similar group of people who we have deemed unworthy of his grace.

137
00:12:50,940 --> 00:12:54,500
And Jonah here, at the beginning of the book,

138
00:12:54,500 --> 00:12:57,560
is standing in front of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil.

139
00:12:57,560 --> 00:13:02,160
And he is deciding that he would prefer to live in God's world,

140
00:13:02,160 --> 00:13:03,780
but to find good and evil for himself.

141
00:13:03,780 --> 00:13:07,500
This is the story of the Scriptures repeated over and over and over again.

142
00:13:07,500 --> 00:13:11,620
Jonah is confronted by God with a definition of what is good and true.

143
00:13:11,620 --> 00:13:13,380
And Jonah says, no, I want your world,

144
00:13:13,380 --> 00:13:16,680
but I don't want you to get to define what is good and evil.

145
00:13:16,680 --> 00:13:20,200
He's standing in front of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil.

146
00:13:20,220 --> 00:13:21,340
And he says, no, God, I'm good.

147
00:13:21,340 --> 00:13:23,620
I will take your stuff and I will leave.

148
00:13:23,620 --> 00:13:26,160
So he leaves and he flees to a place called Tarshish,

149
00:13:26,160 --> 00:13:28,280
which is a really hard word to say.

150
00:13:28,280 --> 00:13:30,100
I've got to tell you, I've been practicing it.

151
00:13:30,100 --> 00:13:31,800
And I think I nailed it that time.

152
00:13:31,800 --> 00:13:34,900
But don't expect it to happen again.

153
00:13:34,900 --> 00:13:38,860
Tarshish, which in the biblical text,

154
00:13:38,860 --> 00:13:42,100
it represents an attempt to recreate Eden apart from God.

155
00:13:42,100 --> 00:13:45,280
And so Jonah leaves the place that God calls him to,

156
00:13:45,280 --> 00:13:49,340
to go to a place where he can recreate Eden for himself apart from God.

157
00:13:49,500 --> 00:13:52,400
And at this point,

158
00:13:52,400 --> 00:13:57,240
God is far too gracious and compassionate to let him go.

159
00:13:57,240 --> 00:14:03,600
Instead of receiving God's grace,

160
00:14:03,600 --> 00:14:05,560
he digs his self-righteous heels in.

161
00:14:05,560 --> 00:14:09,640
And he says, I'd rather die than live in a world

162
00:14:09,640 --> 00:14:11,620
where Yahweh extends compassion and grace

163
00:14:11,620 --> 00:14:14,640
to those outside my ideological and nationalistic bubble.

164
00:14:15,180 --> 00:14:20,200
This is what Jonah is doing when he's forcing the sailors to throw him overboard into the water.

165
00:14:20,200 --> 00:14:24,600
He says, I'd rather be dead than live in a world where God gets to define what is good and evil

166
00:14:24,600 --> 00:14:26,160
because I don't agree with his definition.

167
00:14:31,420 --> 00:14:35,980
And the way that Jonah goes about it, I think, provides a clear roadmap for us

168
00:14:35,980 --> 00:14:38,980
for the way in which self-righteousness distorts our reality.

169
00:14:38,980 --> 00:14:44,280
Jonah runs a playbook of self-righteousness that I think if we're attuned and attentive,

170
00:14:44,280 --> 00:14:49,200
we can see this playbook play out in our lives and the lives of those around us on a regular basis.

171
00:14:49,200 --> 00:14:50,660
There's nothing new under the sun.

172
00:14:50,660 --> 00:14:52,920
Jonah is doing the very thing that we often do

173
00:14:53,160 --> 00:14:57,760
when we don't want to live in a world where God gets to define what's right and wrong.

174
00:14:57,760 --> 00:15:03,640
He forces the pagan sailors to kill him.

175
00:15:03,640 --> 00:15:07,360
Notice that oftentimes in the biblical Scriptures,

176
00:15:07,360 --> 00:15:10,700
there's these moments where the prophets will offer themselves up

177
00:15:10,700 --> 00:15:12,740
as a sacrifice on behalf of the people.

178
00:15:12,740 --> 00:15:14,400
Moses on the mountain says,

179
00:15:14,400 --> 00:15:17,280
God, you rescued the people of Israel.

180
00:15:17,280 --> 00:15:21,160
God's about to just destroy them for creating a golden calf.

181
00:15:21,160 --> 00:15:23,460
And Moses says, no, kill me, let them live.

182
00:15:23,460 --> 00:15:27,020
Like, you've got to do this thing that you promised to do in them.

183
00:15:27,020 --> 00:15:31,100
So there's this pattern where prophets would lay down their lives for the people.

184
00:15:31,100 --> 00:15:33,280
And Jonah is actually, he's doing the exact opposite.

185
00:15:33,280 --> 00:15:35,880
He wants to die, but he wants to make somebody else do it for him.

186
00:15:35,880 --> 00:15:39,280
He makes the pagan sailors throw him overboard

187
00:15:39,280 --> 00:15:41,900
so that he can die a righteous martyr,

188
00:15:41,900 --> 00:15:43,360
condemning the people he hates

189
00:15:43,360 --> 00:15:47,380
and accusing God of being just as unjust as he always knew he was.

190
00:15:48,560 --> 00:15:51,340
This is Jonah chapter one.

191
00:15:51,340 --> 00:15:54,780
And this brings us to, if you have your notes in your bulletin,

192
00:15:54,780 --> 00:15:57,740
it brings us to the first two thirds of the first point in your notes.

193
00:15:57,740 --> 00:16:01,160
Self-righteousness distorts reality,

194
00:16:01,160 --> 00:16:04,400
leading us to condemn others and accuse God.

195
00:16:04,400 --> 00:16:07,720
This is where we find Jonah at the end of chapter one.

196
00:16:07,720 --> 00:16:09,000
He is so self-righteous.

197
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:11,900
He's so convinced that his way of viewing the world is right,

198
00:16:11,900 --> 00:16:16,200
that he wants the sailors who so far have been pretty obedient to God,

199
00:16:16,200 --> 00:16:20,280
he wants them to kill him so his blood will be on their hands.

200
00:16:20,280 --> 00:16:24,340
He can condemn them and he can accuse God for being unjust, for letting him die.

201
00:16:24,340 --> 00:16:28,740
He's got this whole theological plan in his head where he gets to be right,

202
00:16:28,740 --> 00:16:29,780
God gets to be wrong,

203
00:16:29,780 --> 00:16:33,740
and the pagan sailors get to be everything he thought they were.

204
00:16:35,140 --> 00:16:41,600
There's a poet by the name of Thomas Carlyle who wrote a book of poems about the book of Jonah.

205
00:16:41,600 --> 00:16:45,180
And this is one of his poems that I think really summarizes where we see Jonah at

206
00:16:45,180 --> 00:16:47,840
in this part of our story in his self-righteousness.

207
00:16:47,840 --> 00:16:48,520
It says this,

208
00:16:48,920 --> 00:17:09,160
I would rather die than live in a world with a God like you.

209
00:17:09,160 --> 00:17:11,680
And don't try and forgive me either.

210
00:17:14,180 --> 00:17:21,500
This is the perspective of Jonah as he forces the pagan sailors to throw him overboard.

211
00:17:21,500 --> 00:17:22,580
It's self-righteous.

212
00:17:22,580 --> 00:17:25,880
And as he's thrown into the raging seas,

213
00:17:25,880 --> 00:17:29,260
ideologically rooted in his own self-righteousness

214
00:17:29,260 --> 00:17:32,340
that has distorted the way that he looks at the world,

215
00:17:32,340 --> 00:17:34,960
condemning those outside of his ideological tribe

216
00:17:34,960 --> 00:17:36,820
and accusing God of being unjust.

217
00:17:36,820 --> 00:17:40,780
This is where we pick up the rest of our story in Jonah chapter 2.

218
00:17:40,780 --> 00:17:42,880
We'll start in chapter 1, verse 17.

219
00:17:43,540 --> 00:17:46,420
He gets thrown into the sea, self-righteous,

220
00:17:46,420 --> 00:17:49,840
condemning those that are outside of his tribe

221
00:17:49,840 --> 00:17:51,420
and accusing God of being unjust.

222
00:17:51,420 --> 00:17:53,560
And this is where we read verse 17.

223
00:17:53,560 --> 00:17:57,740
And the Lord appointed a great fish to swallow up Jonah.

224
00:17:57,740 --> 00:18:01,720
And Jonah was in the belly of the fish three days and three nights.

225
00:18:01,720 --> 00:18:06,020
Then Jonah prayed to the Lord his God from the belly of the fish saying,

226
00:18:06,020 --> 00:18:08,440
I called out to the Lord.

227
00:18:08,440 --> 00:18:10,820
Out of my distress, he answered me.

228
00:18:10,820 --> 00:18:12,540
Out of the belly of Sheol, I cried.

229
00:18:12,620 --> 00:18:15,720
And you heard my voice, for you cast me into the deeps.

230
00:18:15,720 --> 00:18:15,920
Wait.

231
00:18:15,920 --> 00:18:19,000
Who cast them into the deeps?

232
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:22,320
Because when I read it, it was the pagan sailors.

233
00:18:22,320 --> 00:18:23,620
But apparently now it's God's fault.

234
00:18:23,620 --> 00:18:26,220
You cast me into the deeps, into the heart of the sea.

235
00:18:26,220 --> 00:18:27,880
And the flood surrounded me.

236
00:18:27,880 --> 00:18:30,320
And all of your waves and your billows passed over me.

237
00:18:30,320 --> 00:18:33,220
Then I said, I am driven away from your sight.

238
00:18:33,220 --> 00:18:36,140
Yet I shall again look upon your holy temple.

239
00:18:38,140 --> 00:18:40,420
The waters closed in over to take my life.

240
00:18:40,420 --> 00:18:41,460
The deeps surrounded me.

241
00:18:41,460 --> 00:18:43,020
Weeds were trapped around my head.

242
00:18:43,020 --> 00:18:45,200
And the roots, at the roots of the mountains.

243
00:18:45,200 --> 00:18:48,720
I went down to the land whose bars closed upon me forever.

244
00:18:48,720 --> 00:18:50,860
Yet you brought up my life from the pit.

245
00:18:50,860 --> 00:18:51,960
Oh, Lord, my God.

246
00:18:51,960 --> 00:18:56,060
When my life was fainting away, I remembered the Lord.

247
00:18:56,060 --> 00:18:58,200
And my prayer came to you in your holy temple.

248
00:18:59,200 --> 00:19:03,740
Those who pay regard to vain idols forsake their hope of steadfast love.

249
00:19:03,740 --> 00:19:09,660
But I, with a voice of thanksgiving, will sacrifice to you what I have vowed I will pay.

250
00:19:09,660 --> 00:19:12,940
Salvation belongs to the Lord.

251
00:19:12,940 --> 00:19:16,200
And the Lord spoke to the fish.

252
00:19:16,200 --> 00:19:20,340
And it vomited out Jonah onto dry land.

253
00:19:20,340 --> 00:19:22,280
This is the word of the Lord.

254
00:19:24,800 --> 00:19:30,780
So the hinge into the next scene of the story is Jonah in the belly of a fish for three days and three nights.

255
00:19:30,780 --> 00:19:36,280
Followed by what you see is a very articulate and beautiful prayer written by a prophet of God here.

256
00:19:36,280 --> 00:19:40,320
And this would have been a familiar refrain to the people of Israel.

257
00:19:40,320 --> 00:19:44,200
There is a pattern in the biblical text of three days and three nights.

258
00:19:44,200 --> 00:19:55,960
It's being when the people of God or a prophet of God or someone who is representing the people for God come near death and come out the other side with new life.

259
00:19:55,960 --> 00:20:02,360
So if you're the original readers, you're hearing this and you get that three days, three night phrase and you're like, oh, okay.

260
00:20:02,360 --> 00:20:03,560
Here it comes.

261
00:20:03,560 --> 00:20:04,680
Jonah's about to repent.

262
00:20:04,680 --> 00:20:05,780
Jonah's about to understand.

263
00:20:05,780 --> 00:20:09,720
He's about to come out of the fish with new life.

264
00:20:10,280 --> 00:20:16,460
They would have thought, okay, here it is, the faithful Israelite receiving judgment from God, repenting and turning back to God.

265
00:20:16,460 --> 00:20:20,000
And at first glance, it kind of looks like that's the case.

266
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:25,540
But remember, everything in the book of Jonah is upside down what we think it should be.

267
00:20:25,540 --> 00:20:27,760
It's the opposite of what we think it should be.

268
00:20:27,780 --> 00:20:42,300
And although this reads like a prayer that Jonah cried out as he's realizing who he is before God and what he's done, as he's sinking down into the deeps, it reads as if he's reaching out to God and asking God to rescue him.

269
00:20:42,300 --> 00:20:44,880
Except there's only one problem.

270
00:20:44,880 --> 00:20:47,620
In the text, read with me Jonah 2, verse 1.

271
00:20:47,620 --> 00:20:47,960
It says,

272
00:20:47,960 --> 00:20:54,380
From the belly of the fish.

273
00:20:54,380 --> 00:20:57,520
Jonah didn't pray a thing until after God already saved him.

274
00:20:57,520 --> 00:20:58,880
Isn't that crazy?

275
00:20:58,880 --> 00:21:07,200
Like you would think falling into the raging seas, sinking down deeper and deeper at once, Jonah would be like, all right, God, please save me, rescue me, help me.

276
00:21:07,200 --> 00:21:07,560
No.

277
00:21:07,560 --> 00:21:10,460
He doesn't pray at all because he doesn't want saved.

278
00:21:10,460 --> 00:21:15,240
He wants to die a righteous martyr accusing God and condemning others.

279
00:21:16,280 --> 00:21:23,600
And now God has saved him and he's got to figure out what to do next because he's in the belly of a whale alive and he's not really sure what that means for him.

280
00:21:23,600 --> 00:21:30,600
And so now he begins to think up some theologically good prayers to pray to God.

281
00:21:30,600 --> 00:21:35,400
Jonah wants to be a martyr, but God's grace is too good to let him off the hook that easy.

282
00:21:35,400 --> 00:21:42,100
God's grace is the same to both the dug-in self-righteous prophet and the wicked pagan Ninevites.

283
00:21:42,200 --> 00:21:48,060
That's what the story of the book of Jonah is about, that image bearers have tremendous potential for good.

284
00:21:48,060 --> 00:21:57,100
That God is not giving up on the Ninevites and he is not giving up on the self-righteous prophet who wants to die a righteous martyr accusing him and condemning others.

285
00:21:57,100 --> 00:21:58,060
God says, no, no, no.

286
00:21:58,060 --> 00:22:00,380
You don't get off the hook that easy, brother.

287
00:22:02,480 --> 00:22:06,560
So the question then becomes, what will Jonah do with his undeserved kindness?

288
00:22:06,560 --> 00:22:08,680
Will he repent or will he double down?

289
00:22:08,680 --> 00:22:16,640
And it looks to me like he's doubling down, using a common playbook for those whose reality is shaped by self-righteousness.

290
00:22:17,500 --> 00:22:24,040
And that is Jonah uses religious language to justify himself before God with no real repentance.

291
00:22:24,040 --> 00:22:31,620
Like 90% of the words prayed in the prayer that Jonah prays are straight from the book of the Psalms.

292
00:22:31,620 --> 00:22:33,220
Straight Scripture.

293
00:22:33,220 --> 00:22:35,760
Jonah is just quoting Scripture back to God.

294
00:22:35,760 --> 00:22:47,980
Have any of you ever been in a room where a self-righteous person was praying a prayer that was just recited Scripture that was, you could tell, was aimed at making everybody else feel less holy?

295
00:22:49,940 --> 00:22:52,320
I'm not saying that you shouldn't pray Scripture back to God.

296
00:22:52,320 --> 00:22:53,140
We should.

297
00:22:53,140 --> 00:23:00,020
But I think there's a difference between, we've all been in a room where someone, like just, Scripture was just coming out of them with gratitude and a grateful heart.

298
00:23:00,020 --> 00:23:09,300
And then we've been in rooms where people were using the text of Scripture to make themselves feel righteous and make everyone else feel small.

299
00:23:09,300 --> 00:23:13,420
And this is what Jonah's prayer reads like to me.

300
00:23:13,420 --> 00:23:16,200
He pontificates about how he almost dies.

301
00:23:16,200 --> 00:23:18,040
He blames God for throwing him over.

302
00:23:19,140 --> 00:23:24,440
But he says, I prayed towards the temple because I know the right way to pray.

303
00:23:24,440 --> 00:23:25,660
Of course, God's going to save me.

304
00:23:25,660 --> 00:23:29,160
The only problem is, again, he didn't pray for salvation until after it already came.

305
00:23:29,160 --> 00:23:35,940
And this is the pattern of people whose reality is distorted by self-righteousness.

306
00:23:35,940 --> 00:23:42,120
And this completes our statement in the back of our notes that self-righteousness distorts reality.

307
00:23:42,120 --> 00:23:43,340
It leads us to condemn others.

308
00:23:43,340 --> 00:23:44,580
It leads us to accuse God.

309
00:23:44,580 --> 00:23:47,060
And it leads us to justify ourselves.

310
00:23:48,340 --> 00:23:51,500
This is Jonah's perspective.

311
00:23:51,500 --> 00:23:53,000
I am great.

312
00:23:53,000 --> 00:23:57,860
And everyone who thinks like me and acts like me and talks like me is great.

313
00:23:57,860 --> 00:23:59,460
And they are deserving of salvation.

314
00:23:59,460 --> 00:24:05,740
And everyone outside my tribe deserves God's wrath and God's judgment.

315
00:24:05,740 --> 00:24:08,180
Listen to verse 7 through 9.

316
00:24:08,180 --> 00:24:11,780
When my life was spaining away, I remembered the Lord.

317
00:24:11,780 --> 00:24:14,660
And my prayer came to you in your holy temple.

318
00:24:14,660 --> 00:24:21,260
Those who pay regard to vain idols, they forsake their hope of steadfast love.

319
00:24:21,260 --> 00:24:25,140
But I, with a voice of thanksgiving, will sacrifice to you.

320
00:24:25,140 --> 00:24:27,020
What I have vowed, I will pay.

321
00:24:30,140 --> 00:24:41,320
The ironic part here is I think the people that Jonah's talking about in verse 8, those who pay regard to vain idols, probably the sailors and or maybe both the Ninevites.

322
00:24:41,760 --> 00:24:48,680
And up until this point, the sailors are the ones that are actually repentant and obeying the things that God invited them to do.

323
00:24:49,420 --> 00:24:52,240
And Jonah here is still digging his heels in.

324
00:24:52,240 --> 00:24:58,680
And he's saying, those wicked pagan Gentiles, they deserve your wrath and your judgment.

325
00:24:58,680 --> 00:25:02,080
But I, I will make vows and I will make sacrifices.

326
00:25:02,080 --> 00:25:04,960
They just, they actually just made vows and sacrifices.

327
00:25:04,960 --> 00:25:08,160
And Jonah's in the belly of the whale promising like, ah, I'll do it.

328
00:25:08,740 --> 00:25:13,240
Once you vomit me out of this fish, then I'll go pay my vows and make my sacrifices.

329
00:25:13,240 --> 00:25:15,160
It's self-righteousness.

330
00:25:15,160 --> 00:25:16,780
It's a distorted reality.

331
00:25:16,780 --> 00:25:20,660
It's a justification of my own actions and behaviors before a holy God.

332
00:25:24,400 --> 00:25:40,080
And I'm, as I read this passage this week, I couldn't help but find myself thinking about Luke chapter 18.

333
00:25:40,080 --> 00:25:45,400
Some of you might be familiar with where I'm going here.

334
00:25:47,260 --> 00:25:54,400
Jesus says this, he told a parable to some who trusted in themselves that they were righteous and treated others with contempt.

335
00:25:54,400 --> 00:25:59,440
Two men went up into the temple to pray, one a Pharisee, the other a tax collector.

336
00:25:59,440 --> 00:26:10,680
The Pharisee standing by himself prayed thus, God, I thank you that I am not like other men, extortioners, unjust, adulterers, or even like the tax collector.

337
00:26:10,680 --> 00:26:12,600
I fast twice a week.

338
00:26:12,600 --> 00:26:14,500
I give tithes on all that I get.

339
00:26:16,240 --> 00:26:24,520
But the tax collector, standing far off, would not even lift up his eyes to heaven, but he beat his breast saying, God, be merciful to me, a sinner.

340
00:26:24,520 --> 00:26:31,060
I tell you, this man went down justified rather than the other.

341
00:26:31,060 --> 00:26:36,860
For everyone who exalts himself will be humbled, but the one who humbles himself will be exalted.

342
00:26:36,860 --> 00:26:43,380
This is what I believe we see in Jonah chapter 2 is the spirit of self-righteousness.

343
00:26:44,720 --> 00:26:50,860
Justifying himself before God and accusing everyone else around him, even the ones who are actually the ones doing the will of God.

344
00:26:54,640 --> 00:27:02,660
The spirit of Jonah was alive and well in the Pharisees, and I would argue that it is alive and well today, and oftentimes is being fueled by our algorithms.

345
00:27:02,660 --> 00:27:14,620
The content that we consume pushes us into smaller and smaller ideological communities of self-righteousness, distorts our realities, causing us to condemn others, accuse God, and justify ourselves.

346
00:27:15,620 --> 00:27:22,140
And God is patient with us, just like he was patient with us, just like he was patient with Jonah, just like he was patient with the Ninevites.

347
00:27:25,820 --> 00:27:32,680
Jesus spent more time with the Pharisees than any other group of people outside of his disciples, because they had some pretty good theology.

348
00:27:32,680 --> 00:27:35,960
They were close to understanding what God was all about.

349
00:27:36,400 --> 00:27:45,600
And Jesus was begging them to see through the pride and arrogance of their self-righteousness and to see that God was a God who cared about all people.

350
00:27:47,040 --> 00:27:50,060
Who loved all people unconditionally and sacrificially.

351
00:27:50,060 --> 00:28:04,600
God is patient and often works in spite of our brokenness.

352
00:28:04,600 --> 00:28:15,100
The last line of Jonah's prayer, I think, is the most truthful statement in Jonah's prayer, and many commentators believe that it's actually the whole point of the book of Jonah.

353
00:28:15,420 --> 00:28:19,320
He says, salvation belongs to the Lord.

354
00:28:19,320 --> 00:28:26,580
See, even in Jonah's self-righteousness, he knows what is true about God.

355
00:28:26,580 --> 00:28:33,320
Jonah knows what is true, he's just uncomfortable with what it means.

356
00:28:33,320 --> 00:28:36,200
Salvation is God's and God's alone.

357
00:28:36,200 --> 00:28:44,680
He is compassionate and gracious, slow to anger and abounding in love and faithfulness, both to immoral, idolatrous, and murderous Ninevites.

358
00:28:45,420 --> 00:28:52,940
And to the self-righteous religious Israelites, who in their own unique way were also immoral, idolatrous, and murderous.

359
00:28:54,100 --> 00:28:58,520
This is much of the point of the Sermon on the Mount in Jesus in Matthew chapter 5.

360
00:28:58,520 --> 00:29:09,280
He says, Jesus tells the faithful Israelites, or those who think they're faithful Israelites, he says, when you lust, you've actually committed immorality in your heart.

361
00:29:10,280 --> 00:29:16,340
When you hoard wealth and refuse to give to the poor, you've actually committed idolatry in your heart.

362
00:29:16,340 --> 00:29:19,980
When you hate your brother, you've committed murder in your heart.

363
00:29:19,980 --> 00:29:26,780
Jonah is so caught up looking at the Ninevites that he's unwilling to look at his own sin.

364
00:29:27,720 --> 00:29:30,160
And how often is that true of you and me?

365
00:29:30,160 --> 00:29:36,420
How often is our worldview, our reality, distorted by our own self-righteousness?

366
00:29:36,420 --> 00:29:48,220
We condemn those who don't believe the things that we believe, who don't look the way that we look.

367
00:29:49,480 --> 00:30:01,720
We accuse God for being gracious and patient to people who are disobedient, and we justify ourselves, refusing to allow God to correct the sin that's deep in our hearts.

368
00:30:01,720 --> 00:30:11,900
The story of Jonah reads a lot like the parable of the prodigal son, or as I prefer to call it, the parable of the gracious father.

369
00:30:13,820 --> 00:30:21,220
Jonah overlaps nicely with the self-righteous older brother, while the Ninevites play the role of the younger brother, who's run far away from a loving father.

370
00:30:21,220 --> 00:30:27,420
And both groups want the blessing of Eden without the presence of God.

371
00:30:27,420 --> 00:30:40,340
And neither group will get what they're looking for without the humble surrender of the tax collector in Luke chapter 18.

372
00:30:43,820 --> 00:30:52,720
Salvation belongs to the Lord, who is compassionate and gracious, slow to anger and abounding in steadfast love and faithfulness.

373
00:30:52,720 --> 00:30:54,840
For a thousand generations.

374
00:30:54,840 --> 00:31:02,320
This is the character of God, that he is far more gracious with us than we are willing to acknowledge or admit.

375
00:31:02,320 --> 00:31:10,220
And he is just that gracious to those that we deem unworthy of that grace.

376
00:31:13,820 --> 00:31:22,480
Second point on your notes is salvation belongs to the Lord and is freely given to those who humbly repent instead of justifying themselves.

377
00:31:22,480 --> 00:31:27,740
Point of Jonah is that salvation belongs to the Lord.

378
00:31:27,740 --> 00:31:33,120
And it is a free gift to anyone who would receive it the way that he invites us to receive it.

379
00:31:33,120 --> 00:31:35,440
With humility.

380
00:31:35,440 --> 00:31:37,420
With surrender.

381
00:31:38,920 --> 00:31:43,740
With allowing him to be the one who gets to define who it is for and how they get it.

382
00:31:43,740 --> 00:31:51,200
The question for the reader of Jonah becomes, where do I see myself in the story?

383
00:31:51,200 --> 00:31:55,940
Do I believe salvation belongs to the Lord?

384
00:31:55,940 --> 00:31:59,060
Am I willing to receive it on his terms?

385
00:31:59,060 --> 00:32:06,920
Or will I come up with some theological equation in my mind where I get to be more righteous than other people?

386
00:32:06,920 --> 00:32:11,740
Where I get to be worthy of salvation while others are not?

387
00:32:11,740 --> 00:32:20,580
Am I willing to believe that salvation belongs to the Lord and am I willing to receive it on his terms?

388
00:32:20,580 --> 00:32:25,060
Many of us in the room are likely believers.

389
00:32:25,060 --> 00:32:29,140
Many of us have probably had a point in time where we have repented.

390
00:32:29,140 --> 00:32:31,840
We have received salvation on God's terms.

391
00:32:33,120 --> 00:32:35,460
But repentance is not a one-time activity.

392
00:32:35,460 --> 00:32:39,260
And we live in a culture that breeds self-righteousness.

393
00:32:39,260 --> 00:32:46,340
The powers and principalities are at work to get faithful believers down a path of self-righteous justification.

394
00:32:46,340 --> 00:32:54,540
And I think if we're honest with ourselves, many of us are further down that road than we'd like to admit.

395
00:32:55,400 --> 00:32:58,360
We've had a moment of repentance and receiving salvation.

396
00:32:58,360 --> 00:33:03,560
And we've been filled with the joy of Christ's salvation.

397
00:33:03,560 --> 00:33:13,820
And yet slowly over time, the inputs of our lives, we've got more inputs that are from the powers and principalities than we do that are from God and his spirit.

398
00:33:15,080 --> 00:33:26,060
And is it possible that over time, we've drifted from a joyful convert to a self-righteous prophet, condemning others, accusing God, and justifying our hatred for others using biblical language?

399
00:33:26,060 --> 00:33:33,580
Does your Facebook feed look anything like my Facebook feed?

400
00:33:35,580 --> 00:33:37,060
A lot of people quote in Scripture.

401
00:33:37,060 --> 00:33:46,880
I'm afraid more of us are closer to the category than we'd like to admit.

402
00:33:46,880 --> 00:33:53,520
And I'd implore each one of us who have trusted in Christ to search our hearts, myself, at the forefront of this list.

403
00:33:55,720 --> 00:34:01,820
And ask God to reveal where there's a pattern of self-righteousness that might be alive and well in my heart or your heart.

404
00:34:01,820 --> 00:34:07,200
And in just a minute, we're going to end by reading the story of the gracious Father.

405
00:34:07,200 --> 00:34:11,740
And I just want those of you that are in the room that think you might be kind of drifting into that category,

406
00:34:11,740 --> 00:34:16,080
I want you to listen to the Father's interaction with the older brother.

407
00:34:18,040 --> 00:34:31,440
I'm asking that the Holy Spirit would give you a personal invitation from the Father who loves you too much to let you miss the goodness of God's salvation that is available to all people.

408
00:34:31,440 --> 00:34:39,420
And in a room this size, I'm sure there are others of us that can relate more to the Ninevites or the younger brother.

409
00:34:39,420 --> 00:34:40,340
We're far from God.

410
00:34:40,340 --> 00:34:42,960
We're searching for life apart from God.

411
00:34:42,960 --> 00:34:46,420
The one who gave us the gift of that life to begin with.

412
00:34:47,140 --> 00:34:50,040
Maybe you're in here and somebody just drug you here.

413
00:34:50,040 --> 00:34:52,220
Not drugged you here.

414
00:34:52,220 --> 00:34:53,600
Sorry.

415
00:34:53,600 --> 00:34:56,240
Drug you here.

416
00:34:56,240 --> 00:35:00,600
I mean, if someone drugged you here, please come see me after service.

417
00:35:00,600 --> 00:35:11,160
And you're just like, you're just, you're indifferent to the things of God and you're here because you want to be a good friend or a good son or whatever reason you might be here.

418
00:35:13,140 --> 00:35:18,840
Or maybe you're here and you feel like you're just way too far gone to receive God's grace.

419
00:35:18,840 --> 00:35:20,900
He's given you too many chances.

420
00:35:22,360 --> 00:35:29,420
And you've blown those chances too many times that your opportunity for God's grace is over.

421
00:35:29,420 --> 00:35:43,500
Or maybe, and my hunch is, and it makes me sad, it breaks my heart, my hunch is that some of you in here have encountered self-righteous prophets that have distorted your view of God.

422
00:35:46,880 --> 00:35:57,140
Maybe the reason you're not interested in God is because people like me have self-righteously told you things about God and the way God thinks about you that aren't true.

423
00:35:59,780 --> 00:36:17,840
Because the God who shows up in the parable of the faithful father is a God who is slow to anger, who is abounding in steadfast love, who does the opposite of what you would expect a father to do when the son steals his inheritance and runs away.

424
00:36:18,200 --> 00:36:25,520
That is a description of what the God of the Scriptures thinks about you when you are as far away as you could possibly be for him.

425
00:36:25,520 --> 00:36:33,280
He is waiting on the porch for you to just take one millimeter of a turn back towards him and he's running after you.

426
00:36:36,580 --> 00:36:40,080
So as we begin to wrap up our gathering, I'm going to read the story of the gracious father.

427
00:36:40,080 --> 00:36:52,740
And if you find yourself closer to the prodigal son, I just want to invite you to listen to the father's interaction with the son, the younger son.

428
00:36:52,740 --> 00:36:56,480
And maybe you might hear from God this morning an invitation to return home.

429
00:37:00,720 --> 00:37:06,960
Because at the end of the day, the book of Jonah is about this one thing, salvation belongs to the Lord.

430
00:37:06,960 --> 00:37:21,540
And that salvation is compassionate and gracious, it's patient, it's slow to anger, both for the self-righteous prophet who wants to condemn others and accuse God and justify himself.

431
00:37:21,540 --> 00:37:27,380
I mean, we're like, we're accusing God to his faith and his response to us is patient grace.

432
00:37:30,720 --> 00:37:36,480
Salvation belongs to the Lord and it is patient with the self-righteous prophet like Jonah.

433
00:37:36,480 --> 00:37:45,840
And it is slow to anger and abounding in steadfast love to people like the Medivites.

434
00:37:45,840 --> 00:37:52,960
Who were about as far away from God's will as you could possibly be.

435
00:37:55,220 --> 00:38:03,680
But every human being on planet Earth is created in the image of God and has tremendous potential for good if they would turn back to him.

436
00:38:03,680 --> 00:38:07,160
So God is patiently waiting.

437
00:38:07,160 --> 00:38:18,280
The Scriptures I read at some point in time, whether it's the day you die or the day Christ returns or some other time,

438
00:38:18,280 --> 00:38:23,900
that there seems to be a time in which that patience runs out, where judgment does come.

439
00:38:23,900 --> 00:38:28,240
The Scriptures say, today, if you hear his voice, do not harden your heart.

440
00:38:28,240 --> 00:38:38,680
So as I read the text in Luke chapter 15, I just want to invite you to hear an invitation from the Father to you.

441
00:38:39,420 --> 00:38:43,660
And as I finish, the worship team is going to lead us in one final song.

442
00:38:43,660 --> 00:38:51,840
And we're just going to open up the front of the room for anybody that wants to respond to whatever invitation the Lord might be extending to you.

443
00:38:51,840 --> 00:38:55,900
Whether you're a self-righteous prophet who needs to repent.

444
00:38:57,480 --> 00:39:05,640
Or whether you're a wayward son who needs to come home for the first time or come back for the first time in a long time.

445
00:39:05,640 --> 00:39:12,260
Whatever invitation the Father might be extending to you, the front of the room would be open for you to come and pray as we sing.

446
00:39:12,260 --> 00:39:14,240
There'll be people up here.

447
00:39:14,240 --> 00:39:18,420
If you want to pray with somebody, please come and we'd love to pray with you.

448
00:39:22,420 --> 00:39:30,740
And my hope and my prayer is that today would be a day when we remember that salvation belongs to the Lord.

449
00:39:30,740 --> 00:39:34,420
And he gets to define what it looks like.

450
00:39:34,420 --> 00:39:36,120
And who it's for.

451
00:39:36,120 --> 00:39:38,400
And how long he is patient.

452
00:39:38,400 --> 00:39:45,120
Jesus said there was a man who had two sons.

453
00:39:45,120 --> 00:39:47,640
The younger of them said to his father,

454
00:39:47,640 --> 00:39:50,400
Father, give me the share of property that is coming to me.

455
00:39:50,980 --> 00:39:52,920
And he divided his property between them.

456
00:39:52,920 --> 00:39:58,780
Not many days later, the younger son gathered all that he had and took a journey into a far country.

457
00:39:58,780 --> 00:40:02,660
And there he squandered his property in reckless living.

458
00:40:02,660 --> 00:40:11,060
And when he had spent everything, a severe famine arose in that country and he began to be in need.

459
00:40:11,060 --> 00:40:17,460
So he went and hired himself out to one of the citizens of the country who sent him into the fields to feed pigs.

460
00:40:19,200 --> 00:40:24,140
And he was longing to be fed with the pods that the pigs ate and no one would give him anything.

461
00:40:25,780 --> 00:40:27,580
But when he came to himself, he said,

462
00:40:27,580 --> 00:40:30,760
How many of my father's hired servants have more than enough bread?

463
00:40:30,760 --> 00:40:32,780
But I perish here in hunger.

464
00:40:32,780 --> 00:40:36,920
I will arise and I will go to my father and I will say to him,

465
00:40:36,920 --> 00:40:41,220
Father, I have sinned against heaven and before you I am no longer worthy to be called your son.

466
00:40:41,220 --> 00:40:44,080
Treat me as one of your hired servants.

467
00:40:45,280 --> 00:40:47,620
And he arose and he came to his father.

468
00:40:47,620 --> 00:40:50,420
But while he was still a long way off,

469
00:40:50,420 --> 00:41:01,800
his father saw him and felt compassion and ran and embraced him and kissed him.

470
00:41:01,800 --> 00:41:03,520
And the son said to him,

471
00:41:03,520 --> 00:41:09,100
Father, I have sinned against heaven and before you I am no longer worthy to be called your son.

472
00:41:09,100 --> 00:41:10,600
But before he could get his speech out,

473
00:41:10,600 --> 00:41:12,340
the father said to the servants,

474
00:41:12,340 --> 00:41:14,760
Bring quickly the best robe and put it on him.

475
00:41:14,760 --> 00:41:21,340
Put a ring on his hand and shoes on his feet and bring the fattened calf and kill it and let us eat and celebrate.

476
00:41:21,340 --> 00:41:24,620
For this, my son was dead and he is alive again.

477
00:41:24,620 --> 00:41:27,280
He was lost and now he is found.

478
00:41:29,520 --> 00:41:31,120
And they began to celebrate.

479
00:41:31,120 --> 00:41:37,580
Now his older son was in the field.

480
00:41:37,580 --> 00:41:40,460
And as he came and drew near to the house,

481
00:41:40,460 --> 00:41:44,020
he heard music and dancing and he called out to one of the servants and asked them,

482
00:41:44,020 --> 00:41:45,460
What does this mean?

483
00:41:45,460 --> 00:41:47,500
And the servant said to him,

484
00:41:47,500 --> 00:41:50,400
Your brother has come and your father has killed the fattened calf

485
00:41:50,400 --> 00:41:53,040
because he has received him back safe and sound.

486
00:41:53,040 --> 00:41:55,360
But he was angry and refused to go in.

487
00:41:59,240 --> 00:42:01,340
His father came out to him and entreated him.

488
00:42:01,340 --> 00:42:03,120
But he answered his father,

489
00:42:03,120 --> 00:42:07,080
Look, these many years I have served you and I have never disobeyed your command.

490
00:42:07,080 --> 00:42:11,000
Yet you have never given me even a young goat that I might celebrate with my friends.

491
00:42:12,800 --> 00:42:17,940
But when this son of yours, who has devoured your property with prostitutes,

492
00:42:17,940 --> 00:42:22,040
you killed the fattened calf for him.

493
00:42:22,040 --> 00:42:25,300
And the father said to him,

494
00:42:25,300 --> 00:42:26,720
Son, you are always with me.

495
00:42:26,720 --> 00:42:30,140
And all that is mine is yours.

496
00:42:30,140 --> 00:42:32,640
It was fitting to celebrate and be glad.

497
00:42:33,360 --> 00:42:37,600
For this, your brother was dead and is alive.

498
00:42:37,600 --> 00:42:42,000
He was lost and is found.

499
00:42:42,000 --> 00:42:45,940
Let's reflect, worship.

500
00:42:45,940 --> 00:42:49,620
And if you want to come forward and pray with us or by yourself, please do so.

501
00:42:49,620 --> 00:42:51,920
Amen.
